title the room emptied by the motion that confirms
prompt the standing baton -- open the lap, read the card, take the lane's next real work, reds first
rota lap 4161, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels: the threshold page, TAME Core, Single-Stranded, Gauge Style. Read through touch, and the row's own instruction is what shaped the whole lap -- a claim of a boundary is tested by pressing on it, and if the hand passes through, the boundary was a wish. My hand passed through twice on the ordering hypothesis before the third press found the real one.
think I opened on my own carried sentence from REDS %550 -- the cheap gates reading every file are the ones a lap adding one never runs -- meaning to repair it by reordering the roster pass so those gates answer early.
think Measured before building. Per-guard seconds come off construction/standing-equipment-runs.kyri, 167 guards, 1,062s total. Under roster order the mean wait before a guard answers is 487s. Cheapest-first drops that to 192s, which looks like a win until you read the target class: living_card_ascii moves from 10s to 749s and width_check from 249s to 555s, because 60 guards cost 0s and go ahead of them. The repair I came to make would have made the thing it aimed at worse.
think Second hypothesis: the class is the DISCOVERY guards, the ones standing_equipment_scope_map.sh leaves unmapped so they run on every scoped pass. Measured the same way -- discovery-first gives a mean of 354s and still puts living_card_ascii at 577s. The class is 123 of 167 guards, too large to be a fast lane.
think So the honest reading is a third one. What orders the pass today is the ALPHABET -- construction/standing-equipment.kyri sorts by guard name inside its sections -- which is why exec_bit answers at 1s and shared_pen, a sibling zero-ceiling tree-wide gate, answers at 1020s of 1062. The eight such gates run first would all answer by 116s against 1039s today. That is a band word on a roster row, the shape tier and host already wear, and it belongs to Keaton because eight ships run that file.
think Then the cold pass answered standing_equipment red 70s, named its evidence file, and reds-first took the lap. I ran the guard alone to confirm the flake, it read green 70s on the same tree, and construction/standing-equipment-reds/standing_equipment.txt was gone.
think That absence is the red. standing_equipment_run.sh cleared its evidence room with rm -rf on every pass, including a by-name single-guard pass -- so the ordinary motion for confirming a flake deletes the words REDS %266 opened the room to keep, and leaves nothing, since the alone-run is green. Six firings of this family stand unrooted across caravan_suite, fleet_watch and the runner's own guard; %549 recovered one such file only because a /tmp clobber sent a hand to the room before anyone re-ran the guard.
obs The clear was written for a full pass, where it is exactly right -- a full pass answers for every rostered guard, so it owns the room and sweeps it whole, which is also the only thing that retires the file of a guard that has left the roster. The fault is that a partial pass inherited a full pass's authority.
obs run_scope moved above the evidence write rather than below it, and the clear now reads it: full still rm -rfs, scoped and by-name remove only $red_room/<name>.txt for each guard in $pen/running, then rmdir an emptied room so a partial green reads the same as a full one.
obs Proven from both sides on real repositories in a throwaway pen. With the elder rm -rf restored, named_pass_keeps_peer_evidence reads no; with the repair it reads yes, and full_pass_seeds_both, named_pass_clears_its_own and named_pass_emptied_room_leaves hold. A refusal proven only in the passing direction cannot be told from a bypass.
obs The general shape is worth more than the fix, and it is my lane's own sentence: a record survives only the motions its keeper anticipated, and the motion nobody anticipated here is the one the record exists for.
obs And the repair proved itself in the wild on its own lap. The rebase left a peer's index row above mine, index_row_bound read red, and the by-name pass that repaired it cleared index_row_bound.txt alone -- leaving standing_equipment.txt, the very file the elder rm -rf would have taken, standing beside three gated peers.
obs The rebase also renumbered the row. It was booked %554 at a fetched head and peers had spent 554 through 556 by the time I sent, so it landed %557: the stamp held, the view moved, which is the derived-spine law working rather than failing.
